I understand your frustration. If blur() and tabIndex are not working, it means the **MUI Focus Trap** is aggressively "re-focusing" the button because it believes the button is the most logical place to park the cursor after a modal close.
If you are seeing a highlight/ring, it is because **the button is receiving focus immediately after the modal unmounts**.
Here is the **"Total Suppression"** approach. We will stop the event propagation entirely and remove the button from the focus path during the closure.
### 1. Remove the "Download" button from the focus trap entirely
Change your Download Batch button to use disableRipple and a special ref to ensure it cannot be focused programmatically.
```jsx
// 1. Add this ref
const downloadBtnRef = useRef(null);

// 2. Update your button
<Button
  ref={downloadBtnRef}
  variant="outlined"
  size="small"
  startIcon={<FileDownloadIcon />}
  onClick={(e) => {
    e.preventDefault(); // Stop the event
    e.target.blur();    // Manually blur
    handleDownloadExcel();
  }}
  disabled={!viewingBatchId}
  // This is the key: Force it to be ignored by all focus logic
  tabIndex={-1} 
  sx={{
    borderColor: "#217346",
    color: "#217346",
    pointerEvents: "auto", // Keep it clickable
    "&:focus": { outline: "none !important", boxShadow: "none !important" },
    "&.Mui-focusVisible": { outline: "none !important", boxShadow: "none !important" }
  }}
>
  Download Batch
</Button>

```
### 2. The "Nuclear" Modal Cleanup
The reason it highlights is that MUI's Dialog component tries to "restore focus" to the element that triggered the modal.
Update your **Dialog** component to strictly forbid this behavior:
```jsx
<Dialog
  open={isViewModalOpen}
  onClose={() => handleCloseViewModal()}
  // ADD THESE PROPS TO KILL FOCUS TRAPPING
  disableAutoFocus={true}
  disableEnforceFocus={true}
  disableRestoreFocus={true}
  keepMounted={false} // Ensure it unmounts completely
  fullWidth
  maxWidth="lg"
>

```
### 3. Why this is still happening (The "Ghost" Focus)
If you see the highlight *even after applying the above*, it is not the Button; **it is the IconButton (the X icon) or the Dialog itself**.
When you click the "X" button to close, that button keeps focus for a few milliseconds while the Dialog is fading out. When the Dialog is gone, that "X" button is gone, and the browser then defaults focus to the **next available button on the screen**, which is your Download Batch button.
**To fix this, change your Close Icon:**
```jsx
<IconButton 
  onClick={handleCloseViewModal}
  onMouseDown={(e) => e.preventDefault()} // Prevents the focus on click
  sx={{ outline: 'none !important' }}    // Removes the blue ring
>
  <CloseIcon />
</IconButton>

```
### Final Check
If you apply the onMouseDown={(e) => e.preventDefault()} on the **Close Icon** AND the **Download Batch button**, and you set disableRestoreFocus={true} on the Dialog, it is mathematically impossible for the button to receive focus.
**Try this one last thing:**
If it *still* highlights, open the browser console (F12) while it is highlighted and type:
document.activeElement
It will tell you exactly which element is being focused. If it is the button, the tabIndex={-1} and onMouseDown fix will definitely stop it.
